Group type: medium
Definition: a medium group is a stable structural position a character holds — not something chosen by personal preference. Membership is defined by role, rank, assignment, birth or place, not by who a character personally likes.
Test: could you place this character in the unit from their position alone, independent of who they personally like or dislike?
Include: professional or service units — squads, crews, guilds, orders, departments, agencies, ministries, covens, chapters, or whatever term the universe itself uses for a working unit. Also educational cohorts: classes, years, cohorts, training programmes.
Also include a house or faction inside a larger organization, distinct from the organization as a whole: Hogwarts houses, Gotei 13 squads, a faction within a court. Members of one house share position; the houses may compete with each other, but that rivalry belongs in group_relations, not here.
Also include master-subordinate lineages held by position, not choice: a retinue, vassals, or the line of students under one mentor — a teaching lineage (a mentor, their student, that student's own student) is a structural chain, not a company of equals.
Also include territorial communities where the place itself defines the social bond: a village, a district, a ship's crew, a settlement.
Also include the people who run or serve the institutions the cast belongs to: teaching staff, officers, administrators, guards, servants, crew, retainers. They form a group of their own even when the narrative never names it.
Also include a family or dynasty when position within it matters — an heir, a head of house, a line of succession. Return one only when the family itself functions as a structural anchor in the story, not for every set of relatives mentioned in passing.
Exclude: informal friend groups or cliques with no structural position behind them, and institutions too large for mutual recognition.
Coverage: return every character you can actually name for each group. Do not pad a group to a plausible size: a class of twenty may yield only the eight students the story has actually shown. Listing fewer real members is correct; inventing members to complete a roster is not. Include adult, background and antagonist characters, not only the main cast.
Do not invent membership. A character who genuinely belongs to no organized group is omitted rather than forced into one.
Do not create a residual group to absorb the remainder. Every returned group is a real structure with a name of its own.
